--- (4) READ THE LEVEL VOCABULARY FROM THE REGISTRATION ---------------------
  analyse_t26.py and build_t26.py each hold LEVELS = ("L1","L2","L3").
  `read_registered_ladder` refuses a registration lacking all three rows, so
  T26b_PREREGISTRATION.md -- which registers ONE level deliberately -- is
  STRUCTURALLY UNREADABLE.  The change is to take the level set FROM the ladder
  rows found, and refuse only on ZERO rows or a duplicate row, both of which
  that function already detects.  NOT WRITTEN AS A DIFF HERE: it is a change to
  a GRADING instrument in another lane's file, it alters what every existing
  T26 caller validates against, and it should be a supervisor-routed change
  rather than one appended to a builder proposal.

--- (5) THE COMPANION THE REGISTRATION NEEDS TO BE BINDING -------------------
  T26b section 3.5 registers an ENVELOPE LEVEL.  Nothing reads it: 02c96cd48
  pins `env` to the REGISTERED DUCT-BORE row as a fallback, which is correct
  behaviour but means the registered envelope value is INERT.  A `reg_env_level()`
  reader, defaulting to the duct-bore row when a registration does not carry an
  envelope row, makes it binding.  NOT WRITTEN HERE for the same reason as (4):
  it should be read as its own diff, not smuggled in beside a CLI flag.

=============================================================================
WHERE v2 IS WRONG, NAMED BY ITS AUTHOR
=============================================================================
1. CHANGE (3) MAKES EVERY EXISTING T26 CERTIFICATE UNREADABLE THE MOMENT ANYONE
   APPENDS A DATED ADDENDUM TO T26_PREREGISTRATION.md.  That is the POINT -- a
   build is not certified against a document that moved -- but it is a
   behaviour change that will fire on correct historical work, and the first
   thing it does is invalidate certificates nobody intended to invalidate.  It
   needs a decision about whether an addendum should re-pin or re-certify, and
   that decision is the supervisor's, not this diff's.

2. CHANGE (2) IS SILENT TODAY.  With one registration, `registration or
   REGISTRATION` resolves to the same file and the behaviour is identical -- the
   same could-not-fire shape this lane found in its own env diff. IT MUST BE
   DRIVEN with two documents whose Delta_0 DIFFER, and the control is that
   reg_surface_levels REFUSES on the mismatch rather than silently validating
   against the wrong Delta_0.  A test with one document proves nothing.

3. CHANGE (1) STILL FAILS OPEN ON A WRONG-BUT-VALID PATH.  A mistyped path to a
   real, parseable registration builds happily and certifies against it.  (3)
   reduces the blast radius -- the certificate then at least pins WHICH document
   -- but nothing here refuses a build that cites a real registration for the
   wrong rung.  The honest guard for that is the rung name, and this diff does
   not add one.

4. (4) AND (5) ARE NAMED AND NOT WRITTEN.  A proposal that quietly grew to touch
   a grading instrument would be the wrong shape to hand over, and T26b cannot
   build until they exist -- so T26b stays PENDING on its instruments and that
   is recorded in its section 11 rather than worked around.

COST: none at runtime.
